A||N||5||Dan87GT||V6 acceleration miss||||1||
Z||000000||Dan87GT||06-14-2000||07:48 AM||||when I first give my 86SE V6 gas in misses until it hits about 1000 RPM(in all gears). I have changed the plugs, wires, cap/rotor, checked the EGR and tube, replaced the fuel filter, and cleaned the throttle body. I am stumped! Any thoughts?||||reg||1
Z||000001||BizzaroGT||06-14-2000||03:53 PM||||It could be your throttle position sensor.  I've heard that when they go out the affect a specific area of the rpm band.  The fact that the miss you are experiencing is at the same rpm in all gears could be evidence of this.||||reg||1
Z||000002||86SE||06-14-2000||05:06 PM||||I think I know what it is.  Try unpluging your tach filter.  Its right below the distributor and it is shaped like an M-80 (a cylindrical shape).<P>My 86SE would stumble and miss til it hit about 2000 RPM then it would run fine.  Is your tach jumpy?||||reg||3
Z||000003||Dan87GT||06-15-2000||07:07 AM||||Thanks for the reply, the tach isn't jumpy. I tried again last night, no luck. I have an 87GT that I'm going to start swapping patrs tonight, coil, distributor etc. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Dan87GT||06-16-2000||06:24 AM||||Tried the throttle position sensor, IAC valve, different coil. No luck! it still missis from idle to 1500 RPM, Any thoughts??||||reg||1
Z||000005||ThatRickGuy||06-16-2000||09:12 AM||||I'm not sure about computerized cars, but on the older cars, there were 2 different ways to "advance" the timing, mechanical, and vacuum. The vacuum advance worked off you engine vacuum and helps keep everything running smooth at low RPM, once you get going, say like arroun 1500 rpm, mechanical advance kicks in (the spinning weights under your rotor) and take over. But a bad vacum advance module would do exactly what you're talking about, like I said, I'm not sure if computerized cars have them or not, but they look like a diaphram housing sticking out of the distributer (lower then the plastic cap). removal is just pull the cap and rotor and there will be a screw or 2, the vacuum line, and the lever that attaches to the distibuter.||||reg||1
